\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A fixed line without an invariant complement}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Let #{k=\mathbb F_p} for a prime #{p}, and let a
generator of #{C_p} act on #{E=k^2} by
##{\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}=\bbI+N,
 \qquad
 N=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\0&0\end{pmatrix}.}
Since #{N^2=0}, the binomial identity gives
#{(\bbI+N)^p=\bbI+pN=\bbI}.
This defines a group action, with fixed space #{ke_1}
because #{N(ae_1+be_2)=be_1}.}

\p{Suppose a line complementary to #{ke_1} were invariant.
The generator would act on it by a scalar #{\lambda} with
#{\lambda^p=1}. In characteristic #{p},
#{\lambda^p-1=(\lambda-1)^p}; a field has no nonzero
nilpotents, so #{\lambda=1}. The line would therefore
lie in the fixed space #{ke_1}, a contradiction.
The module is nonsimple and has no invariant complement
to its fixed line.}

\p{The normalized group average of \ref{fdip-00WP} cannot be
formed here, since #{|C_p|=0} in #{k}. Even on the
one-dimensional trivial module the unnormalized group
sum is #{p\bbI=0}, whereas the invariant space is all
of #{k}. These are failures of normalization and
splitting; the simple-module statement of
\ref{fdip-00WO} remains valid.}
